imalCliques/prefix(_:)":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"prefix"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:Si","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Int"},{"kind":"text","text":") -> "},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:s14PrefixSequenceV","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"PrefixSequence"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Self"},{"kind":"text","text":">"}],"title":"prefix(_:)","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/maximalcliques\/prefix(_:)","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/MaximalCliques\/prefix(_:)","type":"topic","abstract":[],"kind":"symbol"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/MaximalCliques":{"abstract":[{"text":"Every maximal clique of an undirected graph’s simple graph once, lazily, each in ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"order (by vertex index): an isolated vertex, or one whose only edges are loops, is a clique of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"one. The order is Eppstein–Löffler–Strash’s: by the cliques’ first vertex in","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"coreNumbers().degeneracyOrdering"},{"type":"text","text":", then as Bron–Kerbosch with Tomita’s pivot (ties to the least"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"index) and branches in ascending index meets them. The sequence holds the graph, so iterating"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"again gives the same cliques. O(n + m) before the first clique, O(d·n·3^(d\/3)) in all for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"degeneracy d."}],"role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/maximalcliques","title":"MaximalCliques","type":"topic","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MaximalCliques"}],"kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MaximalClique